WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.950
- Hi, everyone, welcome
to our daily "Homeroom".

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.810
For those of you who
don't know what this is,

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.370
this is really a live stream
that we started during COVID

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.810
but have kept up and it's still going on.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.670
But it's really a way to have
interesting conversations

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.410
or just to kind of
interact with each other

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.380
and talk about interesting things.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.870
Today, we're actually going
to have an "Ask Me Anything",

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.130
which we've done a couple of times before,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.000
I really enjoy it.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.700
Hopefully you enjoy it as well.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.500
So start thinking of your questions,

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.300
put them on the message boards,

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.420
whether you're watching
it on YouTube or Facebook,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.370
and we have team members who
can look at those messages

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.040
and they'll surface it to me.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.310
But it literally could be about anything.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.610
I like some of the questions

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.170
that are a little bit out of the field.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.590
But before we begin, let me
get my standard announcement

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.745
or thanks to several corporations

00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:03.460
that have stepped up in the run up,

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.950
actually, as soon as they found out

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.994
that we were already running
at a deficit pre-COVID.

00:01:07.994 --> 00:01:10.290
And that with COVID, our
deficit has grown even more.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.360
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.380
AT&amp;T, Fastly, and Novartis.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.650
And even with that help, we're
still running at a deficit.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.500
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.670
please think about
donating to Khan Academy,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:22.770
every donation helps.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.930
And that's what allows us to
keep it free, non commercial

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.650
and reach tens of millions
of students around the world

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.980
who otherwise would not have access

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.440
to really world class
learning in a personalized way

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.303
with interactive software, et cetera.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.650
But with that, I would love
to take some questions.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.963
And so let me see what questions
are starting to come in.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.650
And actually, before I even
start answering questions,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.380
I will give a little
bit of an announcement.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.120
Some of you know that
I've been doing this.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.590
It's not an official Khan Academy project,

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:54.730
maybe one day it will be.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:58.920
It's very complimentary to Khan
Academy, Schoolhouse.world.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.370
And the idea that actually,
it's myself and some volunteers

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:04.720
have been putting that together,

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.810
and that is a way to match
students who need help,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.540
who need tutoring with volunteer tutors.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.250
And it's interesting I've
talked about it before

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.440
where I said hey, if you're
a tutor, you can volunteer,

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:15.330
if you're student,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.010
we actually have more
tutors than students.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.000
And so if you need tutoring,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.440
there's an opportunity especially

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.090
at high school level subjects.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.680
There's an opportunity for free tutoring

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.790
at Schoolhouse.world.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.830
So I encourage and we're expanding it.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.670
It started in Calculus
but now we're expanding it

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:35.280
to Algebra One, Algebra
Two, Geometry, Pre-Calculus,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.230
Trigonometry, or Calculus.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.580
So pretty much any high
school math subject.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.840
If you want free tutoring,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.150
there's a great opportunity
to get it at Schoolhouse.world

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.270
so check that out.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:51.170
There'll be a little form
you have to apply to get in

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.110
because it fill it kind of
a very alpha early stage.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:55.610
But it's a great way.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.670
And you my hope is that we're
able to create a critical mass

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.120
of both volunteer tutors
and students who need help.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.220
And that overtime especially

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.918
as we get into the back to school,

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:06.880
above and beyond Khan Academy

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.730
where you can get help at
your own time and pace,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.070
you can get practice and
feedback and you can get videos,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.500
but there's also a place
to get free in person,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.180
I guess you could say virtually in person

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.350
or over video conference, over Zoom help.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.170
So please, please check that out.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.450
So especially if you're
student incidentally.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.410
So let's see the questions
that are coming in.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.217
From YouTube, Osama al Sadie says,

00:03:30.217 --> 00:03:34.840
"How many hours a day should
a successful student study?"

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.840
And then actually from
YouTube, Sanjay Suknarin says,

00:03:40.366 --> 00:03:44.900
"What helps you study more effectively?"

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.653
And so, these are related questions.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:52.200
I would say anything in life
that you wanna get good at,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.250
if you're able to put in a
minimum of 20, 30 minutes a day,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.810
you would be surprised
how far you can get.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.150
So if you're just trying to let's say,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.370
let's just say take math.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.200
If you're just trying
to get better at math,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.520
you literally do 2030 minutes a day,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.260
you will get better at math,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.360
especially on Khan Academy working

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:10.210
at your own time and pace.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:13.019
Now there are certain things
that just to get started up

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.410
and get into the flow

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.470
takes a little bit more than 30 minutes.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.460
Like if you wanted to
learn to be a programmer

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.810
or become a great writer, but even then,

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.290
it's about regular practice.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.760
It's about just showing
up and starting to write

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.750
whether it's writing a
creative writing story,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.500
or whether it's writing some code.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.850
And there you might do a longer stretch,

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.390
do two, three hours at a time,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.530
or even if you're really in
the zone, do several hours.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:40.270
But once again, it's all
about the regularity of it.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.713
If you have an exam the next day,

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.650
obviously we've all
been in those situations

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:47.400
where we have to cram a little bit.

00:04:48.382 --> 00:04:52.900
And I'm guilty of doing what
I'm telling you not to do.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.680
But I think sleep is very important.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.230
So I wouldn't study into
your normal sleep time.

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:02.150
And the reason is, not
only will you be more rest

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:04.350
of the next day sleep is when

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.940
a lot of those neural connections
are forming in your brain,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.010
it's when your subconscious
has a chance to really process

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.480
a lot of that information.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.133
And so I find that I
could study a good bit

00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:18.060
if I get a good night's sleep.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.110
When I wake up in the morning,
not only am I refreshed,

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:23.720
but my brain actually has
understood the material

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.833
in a strange way a lot better.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.567
So from YouTube Park Shah asks,

00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:32.590
"What do you use donations for?"

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:34.510
Good question, Park.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.240
So a lot of you associate Khan Academy

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.710
with things that maybe I have
done, made videos, et cetera,

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.480
but what you might not appreciate

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.110
and I think I get undue credit,

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:48.290
is that kind of kind of me
is over 200 full-time people

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:49.250
and you might then say,

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.700
why does kind of got me
need 200 full time-people?

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.700
Well, first, think about the
number of subjects and grades

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.151
that we are covering at Khan Academy.

00:06:02.151 --> 00:06:04.910
We have Khan Academy
Kids for early learners,

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:06.770
then we go into elementary school,

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:10.430
middle school and high
school, even early college.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:11.690
That is many, many,

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:16.690
we have over 30 grade level
years of courses, so to speak.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.220
So there's a lot of content there

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.120
that we try to cover as
comprehensively as possible.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.050
It's not just creating the videos,

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:27.580
it is creating exercises for
every skill for every standard.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.270
And when we create exercises,

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.040
we don't just create two
or three per standard,

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.740
we create 20 or 30,

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.280
so that you can get as
much practice as you need.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.280
Most students will not see all
of the items on Khan Academy,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.400
but in case they need more
practice, we have that practice.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.400
Then on top of that, when you have a site

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.900
that is serving 20, 30
million people every month,

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.550
it actually takes pretty
technical sophistication.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.990
Just the server costs on
Khan Academy, pre-COVID,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.480
cost about five, six
million dollars a year.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.270
With COVID, our traffic has increased

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.870
by a factor of two to three.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:04.810
And so then you can imagine
now our server costs

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.310
are on track to be in the 10,

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.920
you know, approaching 10 million

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:11.660
or even crossing 10 million a year.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.220
Then on top of that all that software

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:14.860
that you see on Khan Academy that's free,

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.460
that's non-commercial,
the teacher dashboards,

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.770
the interactive, the game mechanics,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.127
be able to keep track of what you know

00:07:21.127 --> 00:07:22.260
and what you don't know

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.580
and give you as much practice as you need.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.600
In order to write software that can scale

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.780
to that many people,

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.070
you need a very good engineering team

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:32.890
and a reasonably sized engineering team.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.640
We have 80 engineers, and
you might say, 80, engineers,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:36.600
what are they all working on?

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.380
Well think about all of
the back-end infrastructure

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:42.030
to keep something up
and running like this.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.640
Think about the constant
improvements we wanna do

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:45.650
on the user interface.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.220
And you know, like any piece of software,

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.750
there are bugs that we
have to constantly fix.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.620
Then on top of that, think
about the different grade levels

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.470
and modalities on Khan Academy.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.369
We're also the official
practice for the SAT,

00:07:57.369 --> 00:07:59.350
and we're also the official
practice for the LSAT

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:03.010
and then has a slightly
different software experience.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.030
So that's for the engineering.

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:06.110
And then obviously,
working with the engineers,

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.580
we have designers, product managers.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:10.100
And then we have a philanthropy team

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.440
that obviously theirs is to raise money.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.710
So you might say, they're part that

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.360
we do need to pay their salaries.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.620
And then we have like a partnerships team.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.960
Where we need to partner with
folks like the College Board,

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.220
or ELSAC, or other groups to
make sure, and school districts

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:28.440
to make sure that Khan Academy
is having the maximum impact.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.260
We have an advocacy and research team,

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.060
where we're constantly trying to study

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:34.283
how Khan Academy is impacting people

00:08:34.283 --> 00:08:35.893
and how we can get better at that.

00:08:35.893 --> 00:08:39.330
We have data analytics team
that's looking at the data,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.350
oftentimes in conjunction

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:41.890
with the advocacy and research team

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:43.360
to understand how we can get better.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.760
And then of course, you just
need a subgroup of leaders

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.540
to help manage all of this stuff.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:50.930
So hopefully that explains it.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.040
But I will say and this is
kind of, in the spirit of me,

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.440
asking for money if you're
in a position to do so.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.930
Our budget is the budget
of a large high school,

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:03.280
but obviously Khan Academy
reaches many, many, many more

00:09:04.423 --> 00:09:06.230
then that would apply.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.450
A social return on
investment of Khan Academy

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.030
is very, very, very large.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:12.810
And you can do some calculations on that

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.803
however you wanna calculate
it, it's very large, hopefully.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:23.020
So, from Facebook, Patricia Atsinku asks,

00:09:23.477 --> 00:09:26.100
"What is your favorite food?"

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:28.410
So that might seem like a simple question,

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.800
but my wife will tell you,
for me, it's very complex.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:32.220
And there's a lot of soul searching

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.430
because there are things that
my animal brain wants to eat,

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:38.340
that I know are not good for me.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.730
And then when I eat them,

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.300
it creates this whole cycle of guilt.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:45.150
And so it's not clear whether net-net

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.010
there's benefits to it.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.493
So I do have a sweet tooth.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.530
I think I've grown up eating
from South Asian desserts,

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:58.810
that I especially like, I
don't know, familiar with,

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:00.950
in Bengali, we say Rasgulla,

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:02.874
Hindi speakers would say Rasgulla,

00:10:02.874 --> 00:10:07.874
kind of a, I don't know,
cottage cheese, milk sphere

00:10:10.074 --> 00:10:13.160
that's kind of boiled in syrup.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.440
As you can imagine for someone

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.100
who has a family history of diabetes,

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:17.450
that is not a good thing to be eating,

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:18.930
but that is one of my favorite foods.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.620
There's also another South Asian
dessert known as Ras malai,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.900
also one of my favorite,
but not a good thing to eat.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:29.472
So I've been lately trying to
actually cut out all sugar,

00:10:29.472 --> 00:10:34.340
the way I work, I find abstinence
is easier than moderation.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:35.640
That it's very debatable thing.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.790
I'm curious to see whether
y'all feel whether,

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.190
how you feel on that spectrum,

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:40.300
whether it's easier to be moderate

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:41.800
or whether it's easier to be extreme.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.630
But for me, it's easier
to just cut it out.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:45.540
So I've been trying to be more, I guess,

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.130
you could say ketogenic
but in that spirit,

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.463
I've been able to find some good,

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.190
I've been ordering from Costco

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:55.260
and these aren't like some sponsors,

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.072
but I eat one of these
kind of keto chocolate.

00:11:00.072 --> 00:11:03.390
They're kind of like
Klondike bars, with a stick,

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:04.340
but they're ketogenic.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.350
They have like no sugar, no carbs in them.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:08.780
So that is my current favorite food,

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:13.260
that is my daily indulgence so far.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:17.160
Okay, another thing that I
really enjoy is Rose milk.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.580
So take some milk, spill
a little rosewater in it,

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.360
and then if you don't
know what rosewater is,

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.350
you can usually buy it at like
the local international store

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:28.163
or oftentimes a Persian
store, Iranian store.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:30.410
And then I put monk fruit,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:34.140
which is my kind of natural
sugar substitute, very good.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:34.973
If you're not worried about sugar,

00:11:34.973 --> 00:11:36.530
just put regular sugar in there.

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:38.050
That's another one of my favorite drinks.

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.363
But as you can see, I have a sweet tooth.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:46.387
So DLX Coding247 says on YouTube,

00:11:46.387 --> 00:11:48.360
"Do you read the YouTube chat?"

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.070
Well, the very fact that I've
just read your comment implies

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:54.613
that I am able to read some of it.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.153
All right.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.257
Hacker Russo from YouTube says,

00:12:01.257 --> 00:12:02.950
"How can I get a job in this economy?

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.440
How to get a job after college?"

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:08.640
That's a really good
question, Hacker Russo.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.500
I don't know your particular
circumstance or skill set

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.480
or what you're hoping to get
a job in or where you live.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:20.260
But my general advice for getting a job,

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:23.630
when I came out of college,
it was a very hot job market.

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:24.590
It was in the late 90s

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:26.550
and I had a degree in
Computer Science and Math.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:30.270
And so I found it was almost like a,

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:33.450
it was a bounty of opportunity.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.040
Then when I graduated
from Business School,

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.640
that was in 2003,

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:38.020
that job market I think,

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:39.890
was much more similar to the job market

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:41.410
that's starting to emerge now.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.893
That was one of the worst job markets.

00:12:43.893 --> 00:12:45.700
I remember when I graduated
from Business School,

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:47.930
the Career Services said
this is the worst job market

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:51.080
they saw for MBA since the early 70s.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.660
And my attitude towards that is

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:57.005
just keep putting yourself out there.

00:12:57.005 --> 00:12:58.820
When I graduated from Business School,

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:02.200
my then fiance, now wife,
she was in New York.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.240
So I really wanted to work in New York.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:06.270
And I applied, and I really
wanted to work in finance.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:07.720
I wanted to work in a hedge fund.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.940
And I applied probably 100 places,

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:14.060
I probably ended up getting
20 or 30 interviews.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:16.311
And I got none of the jobs.

00:13:16.311 --> 00:13:19.170
(laughing)

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:20.530
It's not a pleasant experience,

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:21.930
but it's just every day I just said,

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.680
Okay, I'm gonna submit
this number of resumes,

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:26.530
I'm gonna write this
number of cover letters,

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:28.650
I'm going to just put myself out there.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:33.650
And just every job
interview, every rejection,

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:35.410
that's a learning experience.

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:38.160
And it took a while
but eventually I found,

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:43.160
there was a guy, I was 26
at the time, he was 33.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.470
He was starting a small
hedge fund in Boston,

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:50.190
which wasn't ideal, because
it was 200 miles away

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.390
from where my fiance lived.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:55.913
But he gave me He gave me
an opportunity finally,

00:13:56.783 --> 00:13:59.130
and so that was probably
the 30th interview

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.020
that I had.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.150
But, if I didn't get that,

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:03.810
I was looking at other opportunities.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.180
I was looking at this,

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.810
I needed somewhere, I
didn't wanna graduate

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:10.260
and just not have some
way to pay off my debt

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.023
and pay my bills and live.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:15.640
So I was looking at
whatever opportunities.

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:18.120
Sometimes you can get very
fixated on a particular industry

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:21.330
or particular job type
as I had with hedge funds

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:23.490
but I realized at the end of the day,

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:25.370
it was very important
for me to make a living

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:26.780
one way or the other.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.300
So even though it's good to keep trying

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.070
at whatever that aspiration is,

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:35.060
I definitely was looking at
jobs that may be paid less

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:38.030
but I still felt I could
learn something from it

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:38.960
and build my experience

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.650
because obviously any pay
was better than no pay.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:45.130
So I would always index on a job

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:48.520
where you'll learn new things
where people invest in you,

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.950
and if they pay decent, or pay fairly,

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.310
then that's great as well

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:55.720
but it's more about especially
early stage in your career,

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:56.820
are you going to be able to grow,

00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:00.050
are you going to be able to
learn important skill set.

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:02.220
Other things that I would recommend doing

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.133
is just be your authentic self.

00:15:05.738 --> 00:15:08.920
If you can show artifacts of
things that you have done,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.432
this is a world where in
2003 when I was applying,

00:15:11.432 --> 00:15:14.372
I think it was pre-YouTube
and all of that.

00:15:14.372 --> 00:15:17.830
Today you could create screenshots
of work that you've made

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:19.720
and explain it and that has two benefits,

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.540
people will see the work
that you've created,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.797
and they can also see you explaining it,

00:15:23.797 --> 00:15:26.700
that shows that you have
communication skills

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:27.670
and other things.

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.974
And so yeah, the more
artifacts you can create

00:15:30.974 --> 00:15:33.600
of your work product, the better.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:35.760
And I think that'll help you stick out

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:39.003
if there's a lot of resumes
that they're sifting through.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:44.637
So let's see from Facebook,
Osama Ismail says,

00:15:44.637 --> 00:15:46.937
"Sal, what are your
strengths as a teacher?"

00:15:49.496 --> 00:15:52.760
It's hard to say that you
ever fully know yourself.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:55.273
But I would say my strengths are,

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:58.240
I usually have a love for
whatever I'm teaching.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.070
In fact, I don't think I would teach any,

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:01.490
I don't try to teach something

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.710
unless I'm personally excited about it.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:06.700
And I think passion and
curiosity is infectious.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:09.170
I'm a naturally curious person.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:10.720
So I don't view teaching as

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:12.820
let me just regurgitate something

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:14.150
and hope the other person gets it.

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:17.260
I view teaching as let me show

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:21.040
or help this other person
discover how exciting this is

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:22.750
and how intriguing it is,

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.780
and how this is connected
with the universe

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.410
and how it helps to understand
our place in the universe.

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:29.354
I would say the other aspects are,

00:16:29.354 --> 00:16:34.354
I try to bring when I explain something,

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:40.903
I try to bring kind of a comfort to it,

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:43.280
a relaxation to it.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.150
So when you watch a Khan Academy video,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:46.930
or if I'm teaching in person,

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:49.440
I try to use the tone that I'm
using now I don't use like,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.980
the next step of this equation
is when the, you know,

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:53.930
that I think stresses the situation

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:55.300
and when folks are stressed,

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.350
it's harder to engage in the material.

00:16:57.350 --> 00:16:58.377
And I would say the last thing is

00:16:58.377 --> 00:17:00.800
and this goes close to the first thing is,

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:05.430
I do believe that you can
understand things best

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:06.690
when you understand that intuition

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:08.980
and when you can see how they're
connected to other things.

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:11.840
And so I definitely try to
do that as much as possible.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:13.610
I do it for my own brain to understand

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:14.560
that's how my brain works.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.610
And I think it's helpful
for other brain as well.

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:21.103
So let's see other questions here.

00:17:24.911 --> 00:17:27.137
So Pronate Hundy from YouTube says,

00:17:27.137 --> 00:17:30.090
"How do I maximize my writing potential?"

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:33.670
I'm not a writing expert,

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:35.610
but I would say it goes to
one of the earlier quote,

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:36.850
you know, keep writing.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:38.010
I think you can't go wrong with that.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:40.970
Just keep writing, keep
practicing the craft.

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:44.390
And then put yourself out
there, show it to people,

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:45.680
get feedback from people.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.870
Obviously, ideally, feedback from folks

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:50.080
who can give you constructive feedback.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.570
Folks, like teachers, other people

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:53.610
who's writing you really like.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.100
And when they give you feedback,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:56.810
it's human nature to get defensive,

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:59.610
I get defensive, at least in
my mind when someone says wait,

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:00.443
you could have done this better.

00:18:00.443 --> 00:18:01.276
But I'm like, no wait, wait, wait, no,

00:18:01.276 --> 00:18:02.109
but I did that for a reason,

00:18:02.109 --> 00:18:04.450
and you don't see how
genius it is or whatever.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:06.080
But recognize feedback is a gift.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:07.440
And you don't have to take,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.150
not everyone's feedback is 100%.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:12.770
There's amazing writers who,

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:14.810
early points in their
life someone told them,

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.810
yeah, you're not that
good at this or et cetera.

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:17.980
Don't take that too seriously.

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:18.970
Just keep writing.

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.460
When people give you feedback,

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.430
really try to listen
to what they're saying,

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:24.320
keep iterating on what you're creating.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:25.580
And I think if you do that,

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.773
you have a good chance of
becoming a great writer.

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:31.663
So let's see.

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:34.397
Passad Tota from YouTube says,

00:18:34.397 --> 00:18:36.600
"Mr. Khan, what is a good topic to do

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:39.350
to expand your creativity?"

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:40.362
Good question.

00:18:40.362 --> 00:18:42.470
I don't know if I have
like any like clear answer.

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.080
I think one thing is that you need time

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:45.453
to truly be creative.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:50.453
In our world today, it's very
easy to get over-scheduled.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:52.920
And when you get over-scheduled,

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.470
you could be doing productive things.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:56.220
You could be learning things even.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:58.740
But creativity really
sometimes comes out of boredom.

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:00.770
It comes out of like,
what do I do with myself

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:01.830
for the next two hours?

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.090
And I think that's a
very important muscle.

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.215
I think other things that I like,

00:19:06.215 --> 00:19:08.980
surround yourself with
other creative folks

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:10.350
who like to brainstorm,

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.590
who like to think outside of the box

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:14.450
or don't even see the box.

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:16.400
And if you don't have them around you,

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.690
seek people out on the
internet, like that.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:21.620
And if you watch a lot of TED Talks,

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:23.600
you'll see a lot of creative people

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.260
and how they think about things.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:27.010
And there's a lot of people on YouTube,

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:29.420
even people who are doing
kind of fun, silly things,

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:30.820
they're incredibly creative.

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.770
And you can tell maybe I
could do something like that.

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.460
But at the same time, build up your skill.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:40.450
You might wanna go into the visual arts.

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.590
To be a great visual artist,
you need to be creative,

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:44.350
and it is nice to have some core skills.

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:45.750
If you wanna be a great mathematician,

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:46.890
you have to be creative.

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:49.670
But it's also useful to have
some core baseline skills.

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:50.960
You wanna be a great writer,

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:53.840
creativity is huge, but it's great also,

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:55.800
to be able to write well communicate well

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:57.550
and have some decent grammar.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:20:01.470
So that's the best
advice I could give you.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:02.940
But there's nothing like
surrounding yourself

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:05.890
with other creative people and
kind of feeding off of that.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:12.000
So from Facebook, Said Elsa Hosta,

00:20:13.397 --> 00:20:14.310
"Thank you for your work,

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.020
how do you find with some
of these needs to be taught

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:17.410
or which aren't taught yet,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.532
how do you keep track of what do?"

00:20:19.532 --> 00:20:20.500
I think the question is,

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:23.453
how do we decide on Khan
Academy what to prioritize next?

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:27.820
We look at,

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:30.500
our vision is to cover all
the core academic subjects

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:32.370
of pre-K through college.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:33.370
So that's the universe,

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:37.203
so that's math, science, social
sciences, and language arts.

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:41.040
Within that, and I hope in
10 years we have all of that,

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.860
but within that we prioritize

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:47.300
what subjects are we
capable of doing well,

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:49.990
especially capable of doing
well given the modalities

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:53.130
that we do we have at Khan Academy today.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:55.760
And what our subject that
we know a lot of students

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.830
have difficulty in or
could use extra support

00:20:57.830 --> 00:21:00.330
or don't have access to
world class materials.

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:01.700
So that's why math was kind of

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:03.580
an obvious first place to start.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:05.280
And then we were moving into the sciences,

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:07.870
some social sciences, it's
kinda going in that order.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:08.767
But that's what we thought about it.

00:21:08.767 --> 00:21:11.420
And we said what's the
core academic subject,

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.810
we view it as something
that most students take

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:17.260
or need to take if they wanna
keep progressing academically.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:20.403
So K-12, that's your math,

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.990
all the way through maybe
calculus or statistics.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:25.460
And then if you go at the college level,

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:26.850
calculus, statistics,

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:28.500
maybe things like differential equations,

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.590
multivariable, calculus, these
are things that most people

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:33.560
that they go into a STEM
career for sure are gonna take.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:35.480
If you think about the
sciences, chemistry, physics,

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:39.027
biology, about the high
school and college level.

00:21:39.027 --> 00:21:41.943
If you go to college, maybe
organic chemistry as well.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:45.640
If you look at the social sciences,

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:48.570
the world histories, the civics,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.920
I would love at some point to do law.

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:53.270
You can debate whether
econ is a social science

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:54.670
or kind of a traditional science,

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:58.820
but econ psychology is something
that many students take.

00:21:58.820 --> 00:21:59.653
So these are,

00:21:59.653 --> 00:22:01.040
we don't have psychology on Khan Academy

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:02.270
and everything else I mentioned we do.

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:03.740
But we don't have psychology.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:06.899
But that is something
that I think would be,

00:22:06.899 --> 00:22:08.440
something that we would eventually do.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:10.770
So that's how we think about it.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:13.727
So, let's see, Minelli
Hernandez from Facebook says,

00:22:13.727 --> 00:22:16.030
"Thank you for Khan Camp 2020,

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:18.650
my three children are enjoying
and learning a lot, great."

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:22.320
For those of you who don't
know what "Camp Khan" is,

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.350
it's a way to keep everyone
learning over the summer.

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:26.430
If you do a web search for "Camp Khan",

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:28.101
you'll see the resources there.

00:22:28.101 --> 00:22:30.750
And it's a way to keep
kids engaged as they do

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.210
get ready for grade level courses,

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.100
which I highly recommend for any students

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:35.270
or teacher out there

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:37.863
who needs to get students
ready for grade level.

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:42.797
So Siddhartha from YouTube says,

00:22:42.797 --> 00:22:45.890
"How to keep focused
on academics in between

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.020
so much going on in the world."

00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:52.120
I definitely empathize
with what you're saying.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:55.010
I think there's so much in the world,

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:58.160
some of it in our locus
of control, so to speak,

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:01.087
we can do something about some
of it and depends who we are,

00:23:01.087 --> 00:23:03.500
there's a degree to what we
can do something about it.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.070
But then there's so much else in the world

00:23:05.070 --> 00:23:06.380
that especially if you watch the news

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:09.130
can make you anxious, make
you worried about things,

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:10.920
but you really can't do much.

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:12.090
Now it's important to be informed,

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:14.750
you don't wanna just
stick your head in a hole

00:23:14.750 --> 00:23:16.900
and pretend like the rest
of the world is not there.

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:19.113
But what I try to do is stay informed.

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:24.010
I try to quarantine, not overuse the word,

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:25.990
reading the news and
things like that to about,

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:27.343
max 30 minutes a day.

00:23:27.343 --> 00:23:29.460
But the rest of the day,

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:31.580
many of y'all know I meditate every day.

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:34.460
That's a way for me to clear
my mind to still my mind,

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:36.140
and then to just focus on putting one foot

00:23:36.140 --> 00:23:40.180
in front of the other and say
what is in my zone of control

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:43.290
that can make myself or
the world around me better.

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:44.480
And then I say, let me do it.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:48.330
And what I find is if
I start my day early,

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:52.130
and I start taking action and
I start having small wins,

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:53.650
then that builds momentum.

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:55.453
So those small wins could be,

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:59.210
I had a good meditation
first thing in the morning,

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:00.495
I went for a run.

00:24:00.495 --> 00:24:02.230
I try to run a mile every day.

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:03.570
So it's not like long distance running,

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:06.100
but I run a mile as
fast as I can every day.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:08.250
Make my bed I do first
thing in the morning.

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:09.440
And so when I build these ones,

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:11.760
if I'm able to make a video
or two in the morning,

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:13.412
by nine in the morning, 10 in the morning,

00:24:13.412 --> 00:24:16.227
I'm like, okay, I made
my bed, I got ready,

00:24:16.227 --> 00:24:20.470
I went for a run, I meditated,
I made two three videos,

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:22.400
then all of a sudden I
say, you know what, Sal,

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.100
you are a productive human being.

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:26.330
And yes, there's stuff
going on in the world,

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:28.680
but just keep at it, you're
making your positive dent

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:30.990
either on yourself or on the world.

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:34.180
So that's how I attempt to think about it.

00:24:34.180 --> 00:24:35.410
But obviously, we all have times

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:38.753
that you just feel a little
bit either bad or just process.

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:42.950
So there's a couple of questions

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:45.527
from Facebook Mayo Withich says,

00:24:45.527 --> 00:24:48.100
"Aways wanted to pick
at your reading list.

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:49.810
What are your favorite writers?"

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:52.548
And then there's another
question Osama from Ismail,

00:24:52.548 --> 00:24:54.157
second question from Osama Facebook,

00:24:54.157 --> 00:24:55.980
"What are your favorite books, novels?"

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:59.280
I've answered this in
different ways before but what,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:02.393
if you look back here, there
are some of my favorite books.

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:06.320
You'll see some Isaac
Asimov right over here.

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.139
I love the "Foundation
Series" for multiple reasons

00:25:08.139 --> 00:25:11.030
that one more Isaac Asimov down here,

00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:14.990
you'll see a lot of kind of
epic space science fiction here

00:25:14.990 --> 00:25:19.030
and I love that because it
just expands your worldview

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:21.980
or your universal view,
beyond the day to day

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:23.010
that we all live in.

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.440
We can all of us get kind
of caught up with like,

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:30.110
oh, gotta pay the
mortgage, gotta go to work,

00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:31.283
go to the meetings.

00:25:32.372 --> 00:25:33.660
There's just the day to day.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.950
Some of its good, some
of it is kind of tedious.

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:41.500
But then, it's great to get
into these universes where like,

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:44.860
Wow, there's this whole, time and space

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:47.740
and what's our purpose and
I feel like it elevates.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:49.450
And that previous
question about creativity

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:52.760
I find when you when you
read science fiction,

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:54.620
and sometimes fantasy as well,

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:56.330
it takes your creativity to another level

00:25:56.330 --> 00:25:57.730
because you view everything,

00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:01.690
you by definition, these types of stories

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:04.740
break outside of the bounds
of traditional thinking.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:08.940
So in that realm, Isaac
Asimov, Dune Herbert.

00:26:14.631 --> 00:26:18.180
Dune is one of my favorite
books, Arthur C. Clarke,

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:20.020
almost anything he's
written I'm a big fan of

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:21.880
in the science fiction realm.

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:23.550
If you go outside of science fiction,

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.960
I love actually Jane Austen,
"Pride and Prejudice"

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:30.533
most famously, I like
Kurt Vonnegut, big fan.

00:26:34.331 --> 00:26:38.270
There's "Candide" by Voltaire,

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:40.130
if we were to go into a
little bit of the classic,

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:41.783
I love Don Quixote.

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:47.963
I strangely relate to
him in a lot of levels,

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:51.640
to talk about someone who's
living in their own reality.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:55.483
So those are the ones
that stick out for me.

00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:00.147
So this Vishva Natarajan,
from YouTube says,

00:27:00.147 --> 00:27:01.567
"Do you ever burn out?"

00:27:04.185 --> 00:27:06.180
Depends what you define as burnout.

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:07.880
There's definitely been times in my life

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:12.000
where I felt tired and stressed.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.290
And I said, I just need a break.

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:17.050
And you know what?

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.617
And it kind of creeps up at you on you,

00:27:20.617 --> 00:27:22.820
you're doing well and then a little,

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:23.990
you're getting a little
tired of the board,

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:25.330
but like, no, this is super important,

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:26.502
I'm gonna power through it.

00:27:26.502 --> 00:27:27.335
And then before you know it,

00:27:27.335 --> 00:27:29.258
you're really not in a good place.

00:27:29.258 --> 00:27:33.410
You're crabby or you're
snapping at people,

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:36.513
you're not assuming
positive intent of others.

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:40.080
Your brain is just kind
of constantly worried

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:41.240
about something.

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:44.940
And I've tried to become much more aware

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:46.410
of when I'm falling into that.

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:48.410
The meditation helps a lot.

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:50.910
I think we oftentimes,

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:52.040
it's a global culture now,

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:55.340
but it's definitely a Western
culture and American culture.

00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:57.500
Push yourself to the limit
your work is everything

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:01.470
and when you are lucky enough
to work on a social mission,

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.030
sometimes I feel like
the work is everything.

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:05.860
There's millions of people
who need the help, et cetera.

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:07.180
But you also have to remind yourself

00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:10.410
no matter what your job
is, if you truly burn out,

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:12.430
that's not gonna help anybody.

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:15.700
So I've been learning to
take it a little bit easier,

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:20.210
have my weekends to really
rest and recuperate.

00:28:20.210 --> 00:28:22.470
There was a time in my
life, even in Khan Academy,

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:24.410
where I would kind of just keep working

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.430
because I am so passionate
about this mission.

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:29.890
But spending two, three
days with family and friends

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:31.220
and just doing nothing,

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:32.950
getting your head into a different place.

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:34.260
Very, very, very important.

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:35.940
And every I get older,

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:37.810
I realized just how much
more important today is.

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:40.900
So highly recommend meditation,
highly recommend breaks,

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:43.630
and giving yourself every
day a little bit of time

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:46.270
to do something that's
unrelated with your core work.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:49.500
And every week, spend
a day or two at least,

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:52.130
to kind of take your mind someplace else.

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:53.020
You have to do it.

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:55.810
And I think you'll actually
find you'll get more work done

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:58.123
in your regular work.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:03.883
So many questions, I know
we're already at time.

